      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "Roofline analysis",
          "definition": "A back-of-envelope performance model that bounds runtime by the max of two things: time to do the arithmetic (limited by FLOPs) and time to move the data (limited by memory bandwidth). The 'roof' is a flat compute ceiling joined to a sloped bandwidth ceiling; where they meet is the ridge point."
        },
        {
          "term": "Arithmetic intensity",
          "definition": "Operations performed per byte moved. Every chip has a characteristic ops-per-byte ratio (here ~300 once you charge half a byte per FP4 weight); your workload has to match it or you waste half the machine."
        },
        {
          "term": "Batch size (in decode)",
          "definition": "NOT tokens from one prompt. It is the number of separate user conversations being advanced by exactly one token in the same forward pass. A batch of 2,000 means 2,000 different people."
        },
        {
          "term": "Active vs total parameters",
          "definition": "In a mixture-of-experts model, each token is routed through only a slice of the network. Active parameters are what one token multiplies against (compute cost); total parameters are everything that must be resident in and streamed out of memory (bandwidth and capacity cost)."
        },
        {
          "term": "Sparsity ratio",
          "definition": "Total parameters divided by active parameters. Confusingly it is sometimes quoted as an expert ratio (8 of 256 routed experts = 32) which differs from the parameter ratio (671B/37B ≈ 18) because attention layers, shared experts and dense early layers are always active."
        },
        {
          "term": "KV cache",
          "definition": "The stored key and value vectors the model computed for every previous token in a conversation. Each new token attends to all of them, so the whole cache must be read from memory every single decode step, for every sequence in the batch, independently."
        },
        {
          "term": "Decode (vs prefill)",
          "definition": "The autoregressive phase where the model emits one token at a time. It is memory-bandwidth starved because a full pass over all the weights produces only one token per sequence — unlike prefill, which processes a whole prompt at once."
        },
        {
          "term": "HBM",
          "definition": "High Bandwidth Memory — the stacked DRAM sitting next to the GPU die. It is where weights and KV caches live. Its capacity-to-bandwidth ratio (~15-20 ms to read it all) is the hidden clock of LLM serving."
        },
        {
          "term": "Memory-bound vs compute-bound",
          "definition": "Whether your runtime is dominated by moving bytes or by doing multiplies. Small batches are memory-bound (weights dominate); large batches or short contexts become compute-bound. The balance point is where you are wasting neither resource."
        },
        {
          "term": "MFU (model FLOPs utilisation)",
          "definition": "The fraction of the chip's theoretical arithmetic throughput you actually convert into useful model FLOPs. Running at the wrong context length or batch size drops it, and it drops roughly proportionally."
        },
        {
          "term": "FP4 / low-precision formats",
          "definition": "Four-bit floating point — half a byte per number. Cutting the bytes per weight both doubles effective bandwidth and doubles achievable FLOPs, which is why the ~300 constant has stayed roughly stable across GPU generations."
        },
        {
          "term": "Blackwell NVL72 / Rubin",
          "definition": "NVIDIA hardware generations. NVL72 is a rack of 72 Blackwell GPUs wired as one coherent domain; Rubin is the following generation, quoted here at 288 GB of HBM per GPU at 20 TB/s."
        },
        {
          "term": "Dense vs sparse attention",
          "definition": "Dense attention has every token attend to every previous token, so KV fetch time grows linearly with context length. Sparse attention attends to a selected subset — DeepSeek's published scheme effectively makes that cost grow with the square root of context instead."
        },
        {
          "term": "Speculative decoding / multi-token prediction",
          "definition": "Producing several candidate tokens per forward pass (via a small draft model or an extra prediction head) and verifying them in one go. It is the second lever on the latency/cost trade-off, mentioned and deferred in this chapter."
        },
        {
          "term": "Amortization (of weight fetches)",
          "definition": "Spreading one fixed cost over many beneficiaries. Reading all the weights costs the same whether you serve 1 user or 2,000, so the per-token share of that cost falls as 1/B — this single fact is the whole reason batching exists."
        }
      ],
