          "gist": "Dwarkesh points out that GPT-4 shipped in 2023 rumoured at over a trillion parameters, and only in the last six months have significantly larger models appeared — was the world simply waiting for racks with enough memory to hold a 5T model plus KV cache for many concurrent sequences (or for an RL batch)? Pope agrees: eight Hoppers gave you about 640 GB in 2022, whereas Blackwell gives a scale-up domain on the order of 10–20 TB, enough for a 5T model plus KV cache. He notes Google's TPU deployments have had very large scale-up domains for a long time, which may partly explain why Gemini's pre-training seemed ahead — though he cannot disentangle that from modelling advances such as DeepSeek's move to more, finer-grained experts. He closes with the two ceilings: active parameters limited by compute cost, total parameters limited by scale-up size.",
          "whyItMatters": "It reframes the three-year plateau in model size as a memory-per-interconnect-domain problem rather than a scaling-laws or data problem."
