          "plainExplanation": "Pope's closing formulation, which ties this chapter to the previous one on arithmetic intensity. Active parameters — the ones every token actually multiplies against — are capped by compute cost, because they set FLOPs per token and therefore dollars per token. Total parameters are capped by the scale-up domain's memory, because the whole expert layer has to live inside one rack to keep the all-to-all fast. The achievable sparsity ratio is the quotient of the two ceilings, so a bigger scale-up domain does not make tokens cheaper — it makes them smarter at the same price, by letting you raise total parameters while holding active parameters fixed. (The ratio form below is a derivation from his statement, not arithmetic he performs on air; for calibration, DeepSeek-V3's 671B total over 37B active is about 18x.)",
