          "name": "Is a pipeline cut free? The scale-up / scale-out time ratio",
          "plainExplanation": "At a rack boundary you send each token's activation across the slow inter-rack (scale-out) link exactly once. Inside each rack, every MoE layer sends that same token to each of its top-k experts and pulls the results back, over the fast intra-rack (scale-up) link. So the intra-rack fabric moves 2 x E x L times as many bytes as the rack hop does, while being 8x faster. Multiply the two and you get the ratio of time spent on each fabric. If it is at or above 1, the rack hop finishes before the all-to-all does and pipelining is effectively free. Check the arithmetic with his own numbers: E = 8, L = 1 gives (1/8) x 2 x 8 x 1 = 2, comfortably above 1 with 2x margin — one layer per rack works, exactly as he concludes. One wrinkle: he says 'it could be 8 by itself, then we can increase the number of layers per stage a lot until we satisfy this', which implies top-k = 8 alone is not sufficient. Once the factor of 2 he had just accepted is included it already is (8 x 2 = 16 > 8); the layers-per-stage knob is spare headroom, not a requirement. It becomes a requirement for low top-k models: Mixtral-style top-2 gives (1/8) x 2 x 2 x L, which needs L >= 2 layers per stage. Note also that the model dimension and the numeric precision cancel out of the ratio entirely.",
