      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "Scale-up domain",
          "definition": "The set of accelerators wired together by the fast, fully-connected in-rack fabric (NVLink and its switches on an NVL72-class rack). Every GPU can talk to every other at full bandwidth, which is what an MoE all-to-all needs."
        },
        {
          "term": "Scale-out network",
          "definition": "The slower network between racks — InfiniBand or Ethernet. Reiner takes it to be about 8x lower bandwidth than scale-up, which is the single penalty a pipeline cut has to pay."
        },
        {
          "term": "All-to-all",
          "definition": "The MoE communication pattern: every GPU sends each token to whichever GPUs hold that token's chosen experts (dispatch), and receives the results back (combine). It is bandwidth-hungry and irregular, so it wants full connectivity."
        },
        {
          "term": "Expert parallelism",
          "definition": "Splitting an MoE layer's experts across GPUs, so each GPU holds a few experts and tokens are routed to them. Generates the all-to-all, so it belongs inside one rack."
        },
        {
          "term": "Tensor parallelism",
          "definition": "Splitting individual matrices (along the model dimension or the feed-forward dimension) across chips, so every matmul needs a collective. Reiner says the trend to smaller experts has made it much less relevant."
        },
        {
          "term": "Pipeline parallelism",
          "definition": "Splitting the model by layer depth: rack 1 holds layers 1-k, rack 2 the next k, and so on, with activations passed forward across racks. Only one activation vector per token crosses each boundary."
        },
        {
          "term": "Pipeline stage",
          "definition": "The block of consecutive layers assigned to one rack. 'Layers per stage' is the tuning knob in his bandwidth inequality."
        },
        {
          "term": "Pipeline bubble",
          "definition": "Idle time on a rack while it waits for work from upstream or downstream stages. With P stages and one batch in flight, each rack idles (P-1)/P of the time."
        },
        {
          "term": "Micro-batch",
          "definition": "A slice of the global training batch pushed through the pipeline on its own so that different stages can be busy simultaneously. Many micro-batches in flight shrink the bubble but reduce how far each weight read is amortized."
        },
        {
          "term": "Zero bubble / one-forward-one-backward (1F1B)",
          "definition": "Pipeline schedules that interleave forward and backward passes — and in the zero-bubble case defer the weight-gradient computation — so idle slots get filled with useful work."
        },
        {
          "term": "Weight gradient step",
          "definition": "The half of the backward pass that computes dLoss/dWeights, as opposed to the half that propagates dLoss/dActivations to the previous layer. It has no downstream dependency, so it can be shoved into a pipeline bubble."
        },
        {
          "term": "Activated experts (top-k)",
          "definition": "How many of an MoE layer's experts a single token is actually routed through. DeepSeek-V3 uses 8 routed experts (plus 1 shared) out of 256; this number is the main traffic-expansion term in the chapter's inequality."
        },
        {
          "term": "DFF",
          "definition": "The hidden (feed-forward / intermediate) dimension of an MLP block, d_ff. One of the four dimensions along which a model scales and could in principle be sharded."
        },
        {
          "term": "Model dimension",
          "definition": "d_model, the width of the residual stream — the size of the activation vector that crosses a pipeline boundary. It cancels out of the scale-up/scale-out ratio."
        },
        {
          "term": "HBM",
          "definition": "High Bandwidth Memory, the stacked DRAM sitting next to each GPU die. It is the expensive, capacity-limited memory that holds weights and KV cache, and it is what Reiner argues could be reduced if you were willing to pipeline."
        },
        {
          "term": "Blackwell rack (NVL72)",
          "definition": "NVIDIA's rack-scale unit: 72 Blackwell GPUs in one NVLink scale-up domain, roughly 14-21 TB of HBM depending on generation, plus about 17 TB of Grace LPDDR."
        },
        {
          "term": "Prefill vs decode",
          "definition": "The two phases of serving: prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel (compute-bound), decode emits one token at a time (memory-bandwidth-bound). Dwarkesh asks whether they have different rack-locality needs; Reiner says the parallelism pattern, not the phase, is what decides."
        },
        {
          "term": "RL generation",
          "definition": "The rollout phase of reinforcement-learning training, where the model generates its own samples — an inference workload embedded inside a training pipeline."
        },
        {
          "term": "Amortizing the weight load",
          "definition": "Reading a layer's weights from HBM once and using them for many sequences at once, so the memory-bandwidth cost per token falls with batch size. Micro-batching splits the batch and therefore spreads each weight read over fewer sequences."
        },
        {
          "term": "Cross-layer residual / attending to layers a few back",
          "definition": "Architectures (Kimi is the example given) where a layer reads state from several layers earlier rather than only the immediately preceding one. Such links straddle pipeline stage boundaries and make a clean per-layer rack cut awkward."
        }
      ],
