          "name": "Per-GPU memory capacity requirement",
          "formula": "c_mem = (N_total + B · L · bytes_per_token) / (E · P)",
          "plainExplanation": "Two things must live in HBM during inference: the model weights and the KV cache. Weight demand is total parameters (all of them — sparsity does not help capacity, only compute), and KV demand is the number of concurrent sequences times how long each one is times the bytes of key/value state each token costs across all layers. Both get divided twice: by E, the number of GPUs inside a rack that an expert layer is sharded across, and by P, the number of racks that successive layers are spread across. The division by E is exact for weights (each GPU holds its own experts) and Reiner hand-waves that contexts can be sharded the same way; the division by P is exact for both, because a rack holding 1/P of the layers holds 1/P of the weights and 1/P of every sequence's KV. The whole chapter is an interrogation of whether that P is real.",
