          "name": "Bubble-free decode fixes the global batch at B = P · b",
          "formula": "B = M · b, and the minimum bubble-free M equals P, so B = P · b",
          "plainExplanation": "In decode, generating a token for a sequence immediately creates the need for the next token of that same sequence, so a micro-batch that exits the last pipeline stage loops straight back into the first — the diagram wraps around rather than terminating. To leave no rack idle you need enough independent micro-batches in flight to cover every stage at once; with fewer, a stage sits empty during the wrap-around (the pipeline bubble), and with more you gain nothing. Reading it off the whiteboard ('a proof by visual'), the minimum count equals the number of stages: 4 stages, 4 micro-batches. Crucially the micro-batch SIZE b does not change — it stays pinned at the hardware-determined ~300 x sparsity (~2,000) from the roofline analysis, because that is the point where compute time balances weight-load time. So adding a pipeline stage does not split the batch finer; it multiplies the number of full-size batches resident in the system. (The transcript at 1:10:10 says 'the micro-batch size here ends up being equal to the number of pipeline stages' — that is a mis-statement of his own correct claim thirty seconds earlier that it is the micro-batch COUNT that equals the stage count.)",
