          "formula": "t_token = t_compute + P · t_hop",
          "plainExplanation": "Because decode is sequential — stage 2 cannot start on a token until stage 1 has finished it — the network hops between racks add rather than overlap. Each hop runs GPU/TPU -> network card -> top-of-rack switch -> (possibly a datacenter switch) -> the other rack and the same in reverse, and since decode loops back from the last stage to the first, a 4-stage pipeline pays roughly 4 hops per token. Reiner's arithmetic: about 2 ms per hop times 4-ish stages, which he and Dwarkesh round to '10 milliseconds per token', taking a 20 ms step to 30 ms. Checking it: 2 x 4 = 8 ms, so the strict figure is 28 ms rather than 30 — the rounding is generous by about 2 ms, but the conclusion (a ~1.5x latency hit) survives. Reiner is candid that the per-hop number is soft: 'a few milliseconds, but it could be off by an order of magnitude'. Note this qualifies the previous chapter's claim that pipelining is latency-neutral for inference: that was true for compute time, and ignores network hops.",
