      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "Pipeline parallelism (P)",
          "definition": "Splitting a model's layers across separate racks so each rack holds only a slice of the network. A token walks through rack 1, then rack 2, and so on. It divides weight storage but not the work per token."
        },
        {
          "term": "Pipeline stage",
          "definition": "One rack's worth of consecutive layers. With 4 stages, each rack holds a quarter of the model's layers and a token must traverse all four."
        },
        {
          "term": "Pipeline bubble",
          "definition": "Idle time on a rack while it waits for work to arrive from an upstream stage. Avoided by keeping at least as many independent micro-batches in flight as there are stages."
        },
        {
          "term": "Micro-batch vs global batch",
          "definition": "A micro-batch is the group of sequences processed by one forward pass through one stage (here ~2,000). The global batch B is all micro-batches resident in the pipeline at once — with 4 stages, four times as many sequences."
        },
        {
          "term": "Expert parallelism (E)",
          "definition": "Putting different MoE experts on different GPUs within a rack, so each GPU holds and serves only its own experts. The dominant — nearly the only — parallelism used in frontier inference."
        },
        {
          "term": "Tensor parallelism",
          "definition": "Splitting a single weight matrix (here, a single expert) across multiple chips. Reiner declares it obsolete for MoE inference: modern fine-grained experts are too small for the split to pay for its communication."
        },
        {
          "term": "Scale-up domain",
          "definition": "The set of GPUs joined by the fast internal fabric (NVLink on Nvidia) that behave as one tightly-coupled machine — 8 on Hopper, 72 on a Blackwell NVL72 rack, ~500 promised on Rubin. Distinct from the roughly 8x slower scale-out network between racks."
        },
        {
          "term": "KV cache",
          "definition": "The per-token key and value vectors from attention, stored for every token of every live sequence so that generating the next token doesn't require recomputing the past. It grows with concurrency x context length and, unlike weights, cannot be shared between sequences."
        },
        {
          "term": "Decode",
          "definition": "The one-token-at-a-time generation phase, as opposed to prefill (processing the prompt). Decode is sequential and memory-bandwidth-bound, which is why per-hop network latencies stack instead of overlapping."
        },
        {
          "term": "HBM",
          "definition": "High Bandwidth Memory — the stacked DRAM sitting on the GPU package. It is what the memory shortage is about, and its capacity (how much you can hold) and its bandwidth (how fast you can stream it) are separate binding constraints."
        },
        {
          "term": "Sparsity (ratio)",
          "definition": "Total parameters divided by active parameters per token in a mixture-of-experts model. DeepSeek's is about 8. Higher sparsity means less compute per token but more total memory, and it raises the optimal batch size proportionally."
        },
        {
          "term": "Arithmetic intensity (the '300')",
          "definition": "A hardware constant: roughly how many arithmetic operations a chip can do per byte read from memory (~300 across recent GPU generations). It sets the batch size at which compute time and weight-load time balance — hence the ~300 x sparsity rule for micro-batch size."
        },
        {
          "term": "t_mem (memory time)",
          "definition": "The time to stream the required bytes out of HBM, i.e. bytes divided by usable bandwidth. For weights this is N_total / (scale-up size x per-GPU bandwidth), and it is a floor on decode step latency."
        },
        {
          "term": "'Activations' (as used here)",
          "definition": "Reiner says 'activations' at 1:11:12 but in this inference context means the KV cache — the per-sequence state that must persist between forward passes. There are no stored backprop activations in inference."
        },
        {
          "term": "Top-of-rack switch",
          "definition": "The network switch at the top of a server rack that aggregates traffic from the rack's machines. Leaving a rack means GPU to network card to top-of-rack switch, possibly onward to a datacenter switch, then the reverse on the far side — each layer adding latency."
        },
        {
          "term": "Roofline analysis",
          "definition": "A back-of-envelope performance model in which a kernel's runtime is the maximum of its compute time and its memory-transfer time. It is the analytic frame underlying every equation in this chapter."
        }
      ],
