          "derivationSteps": [
            {
              "step": "Write down what has to live in HBM",
              "explanation": "Two things, and only two: every parameter — all of them, because sparsity buys compute not capacity — plus the KV cache, which is batch size x context length x bytes per token."
            },
            {
              "step": "Divide by the two parallelism extents",
              "explanation": "c_mem = (N_total + B·L·bytes)/(E·P). E is expert parallelism inside a rack (worked example 64 GPUs); P is the number of racks holding successive layer groups (worked example 4). Weights shard exactly across both; Reiner explicitly hand-waves that the contexts shard the same way."
            },
            {
              "step": "Now substitute the batch you are forced to run",
              "explanation": "From the pipeline diagram, bubble-free decode requires M = P micro-batches in flight, each of the hardware-fixed size b ≈ 2,000. So B = P·b — the global batch is proportional to pipeline depth."
            },
            {
              "step": "Split the sum and look at the second term",
              "explanation": "c_mem = N_total/(E·P) + (P·b·L·bytes)/(E·P). The P from the batch sits upstairs, the P from layer-sharding sits downstairs, and they cancel outright: KV per GPU = b·L·bytes/E, with no P in it anywhere."
            },
            {
              "step": "Check the magnitudes",
              "explanation": "1T params at 1 byte over E = 64: weights are 15.6 GB at P=1, 7.8 at P=2, 3.9 at P=4, 2.0 at P=8. KV at b = 2,000, L = 100k, 100 KB/token is 312 GB at every single one of those. The KV share climbs from 95% to 99%."
            },
            {
              "step": "So Dwarkesh's objection is right and still loses",
              "explanation": "Yes — a rack holding a quarter of the layers stores only a quarter of each sequence's KV. But you must run four times as many sequences to keep four racks busy. The 1/P saving and the xP concurrency cost cancel identically, and no saving per GPU survives."
            },
            {
              "step": "Which is the second failure to amortise the KV cache",
              "explanation": "'First, we established you can't amortize KV caches across batch size. Now we're saying you also can't shard it across pipeline stages.' Weights can be spread; the KV cache structurally cannot. That is why context length, not model size, is the expensive axis."
            },
            {
              "step": "And the part that does work is finished by P = 2",
              "explanation": "At two stages the weight term is already down to 2.4% of the footprint. Every stage after that reduces total per-GPU memory by essentially nothing — while costing a network hop per token. Hence: none, or maybe two."
            }
          ]
