          "plainExplanation": "When total cost is one term that falls as you turn a knob plus one term that rises, the minimum usually sits where the two terms are equal in magnitude. Check the canonical case: f(x) = a/x + bx has f'(x) = -a/x^2 + b = 0 at x = sqrt(a/b), where both terms equal sqrt(ab) exactly. For e^x + e^-x the minimum is at x = 0 where both equal 1. The general result is that for f(x) = a x^-p + b x^q the two terms at the optimum satisfy A/B = q/p, so 'equal at the minimum' is exact only when the two exponents match, and off by the exponent ratio otherwise. Pope is upfront that he is asserting this rather than deriving it from the actual scaling-law exponents, which he does not have.",
