      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "Chinchilla-optimal",
          "definition": "The DeepMind 2022 result that, for a fixed training-compute budget, loss is minimised at roughly 20 training tokens per model parameter — a much smaller model on much more data than was then standard. It optimises training compute only, and says nothing about serving cost."
        },
        {
          "term": "Over-training",
          "definition": "Deliberately training a model on far more tokens than Chinchilla recommends for its size. Each training FLOP buys less quality, but the resulting model is smaller and therefore permanently cheaper to serve, so total lifetime compute per unit of user value goes down."
        },
        {
          "term": "6ND",
          "definition": "The standard estimate that a training run costs about 6 x (parameters) x (tokens) FLOPs — 2 FLOPs per parameter per token forward, 4 backward. Inference is the '2ND' half of it."
        },
        {
          "term": "Active parameters",
          "definition": "In a Mixture-of-Experts model, the parameters actually used for any one token (the routed experts plus shared layers), as distinct from the far larger total parameter count. FLOP cost scales with active, not total, parameters."
        },
        {
          "term": "MFU (Model FLOPs Utilization)",
          "definition": "The fraction of a chip's peak arithmetic throughput a workload actually achieves. Training can hit 40-50%; autoregressive decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and hits far less, which is why RL and inference tokens cost more machine time per FLOP."
        },
        {
          "term": "Decode",
          "definition": "The token-by-token generation phase of inference, where each step reads the whole weight set to produce one token. Contrast with prefill/prompt processing, which batches the whole input at once and is compute-bound."
        },
        {
          "term": "Rollout / trajectory",
          "definition": "One complete generated sample from the model during reinforcement learning — the model talks to itself or an environment, and the resulting sequence is scored. Generating rollouts is inference work; only some of them get backpropagated through."
        },
        {
          "term": "RL generation",
          "definition": "The inference workload inside a reinforcement-learning training run. It uses the same pre-trained weights as user-facing serving, which is why it belongs in the same compute-amortisation equation."
        },
        {
          "term": "Backward pass",
          "definition": "The gradient computation that actually updates weights, costing about twice the forward pass. If an RL rollout is generated but discarded (low reward, filtered, off-policy), it incurs the forward cost with no backward cost — hence RL's 2-to-6 coefficient."
        },
        {
          "term": "Amortisation (of training compute)",
          "definition": "Spreading the one-off training cost across all the tokens the model will ever serve. The more traffic a model will handle, the more it pays to spend extra training compute making it small and cheap per token."
        },
        {
          "term": "Power law",
          "definition": "A relationship of the form y = a·x^k. Neural scaling laws are empirically power laws in parameters, data and compute — which is what gives the 'costs are equal at the optimum' heuristic its (approximate) validity."
        },
        {
          "term": "D_Chinchilla",
          "definition": "Shorthand used on the whiteboard for the pre-training token count Chinchilla's scaling law would recommend for a given model size — the denominator of the over-training ratio."
        }
      ],
