      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Output tokens cost ~5x more than input tokens because generating text is computationally harder than reading it.",
          "correction": "They do exactly the same weight matrix multiplies per token. The difference is amortisation: prefill pushes thousands of tokens through one fetch of the weights and KV cache, decode pushes one. Cost per token is t / L_pass, so the memory term decays as 1/L_pass while the compute term stays flat. The 5x price gap is therefore a direct measurement of how memory-bandwidth-bound decode is — roughly 5x, meaning ~80% of the chip's FLOPs are idle during generation."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Long context is expensive because attention is quadratic — O(L^2) FLOPs.",
          "correction": "The quadratic term is real and Pope keeps it in the model, but its slope is tiny against the flat cost of multiplying by ~100B activated parameters per token. The binding constraint at today's lengths is linear, not quadratic: streaming ~2 KB of KV cache per context token out of HBM on every decode step, plus HBM capacity. Compute is not what stopped context at 200k."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Sparse attention will unlock 100-million-token context windows.",
          "correction": "Published sparse attention schemes buy roughly a square-root factor, not an unbounded one, and Pope notes it may already be priced into current offerings. Go too sparse and you attend to too small a subset and quality degrades. He sees no good path past the memory wall: 'The HBM is where it is.'"
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Prompt caching is cheap because the provider keeps your KV cache sitting in GPU memory.",
          "correction": "HBM is the worst place to park idle KV — occupying it strands an entire GPU, which is why the hold cost is bytes/HBM_capacity x GPU dollars per second. HBM's drain time is only ~20 ms, so it cannot rationally back a 5-minute TTL. The drain-time argument points at flash or even spinning disk for the long-lived tiers."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Writing to the prompt cache is a discount you take up front.",
          "correction": "Cache writes cost MORE than ordinary input — $6.25 vs $5 per million tokens in Pope's example, a 25% premium. You pay extra to store, and only recover it if you hit, at which point reads are ~10x cheaper than recomputing."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "The 200k pricing tier is an arbitrary commercial segmentation boundary.",
          "correction": "It plausibly marks the physical kink where KV-cache fetch time overtakes matrix-multiply time as the binding cost. That is what lets Pope invert it: assuming the crossover sits at 200k, the batch size cancels and the equation solves for the one architectural unknown, ~1667 bytes of KV per token."
        }
      ],
      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "KV cache",
          "definition": "The stored key and value vectors the model computed for every earlier token in the context. During generation the new token must attend to all of them, so every KV byte gets re-read from memory on every single decode step. It is the thing that makes long context expensive."
        },
        {
          "term": "Prefill vs decode",
          "definition": "Prefill is processing a chunk of already-known tokens (your prompt, a file, a tool result) all at once; decode is generating one new token at a time. They run identical weight matrices — the only difference is how many tokens share the cost of one memory fetch."
        },
        {
          "term": "Roofline analysis",
          "definition": "Modelling runtime as the maximum of two lower bounds — time to move the bytes and time to do the multiplies — rather than trying to predict it exactly. Crude, but it predicts which of the two is limiting, which is what determines cost."
        },
        {
          "term": "Memory bandwidth bound",
          "definition": "The regime where the chip finishes its arithmetic and then sits idle waiting for data to arrive from HBM. Pope's 5x figure means roughly 80% of a GPU's FLOPs go unused during decode."
        },
        {
          "term": "HBM",
          "definition": "High Bandwidth Memory — the fast DRAM stacked next to the GPU die. Very fast (multiple TB/s) and very expensive, so there is not much of it. Capacity and bandwidth are the two walls this chapter keeps hitting."
        },
        {
          "term": "DDR",
          "definition": "Conventional host system memory attached to the CPU. Roughly an order of magnitude slower than HBM but far larger and cheaper per byte; NVIDIA's rack designs ship with both."
        },
        {
          "term": "Drain time",
          "definition": "Capacity divided by bandwidth — how long it takes to read an entire memory device once. Pope's key result is that this is also the economically optimal length of time to store something in that tier."
        },
        {
          "term": "Rematerialisation (remat)",
          "definition": "Throwing away cached state and recomputing it from scratch when you need it again. Here: deleting a KV cache and re-running the forward pass over the original token IDs, which are tiny by comparison."
        },
        {
          "term": "d_head",
          "definition": "The vector dimension of one attention head, typically 128. Not the number of heads — Pope has to correct himself on this mid-explanation."
        },
        {
          "term": "KV heads vs Q heads",
          "definition": "KV heads hold the stored representations of past tokens and live in memory; Q (query) heads belong to the token currently being generated and are transient. Grouped-query attention shrinks the KV head count (to 1-8) while keeping many Q heads, which is what makes the KV cache small."
        },
        {
          "term": "Grouped-query attention (GQA)",
          "definition": "Having many query heads share a small number of key/value heads. The standard trick for cutting KV cache size without losing much quality."
        },
        {
          "term": "Flash attention",
          "definition": "An attention implementation that keeps the intermediate attention matrix in on-chip SRAM instead of writing it to HBM. Why prefill adds no measurable memory-bandwidth cost in Pope's model."
        },
        {
          "term": "Activated parameters",
          "definition": "In a mixture-of-experts model, the subset of weights any single token actually routes through. Compute scales with this; memory capacity scales with the much larger total parameter count."
        },
        {
          "term": "Sparse attention",
          "definition": "Attending to a selected subset of past tokens rather than all of them. Published DeepSeek variants buy roughly a square-root improvement in the KV term — substantial, but bounded, and quality degrades if pushed too far."
        },
        {
          "term": "Cache TTL",
          "definition": "The time-to-live you select when writing to a prompt cache (typically 5 minutes or 1 hour), which is priced differently. Pope reads this menu as revealing which physical storage tier the provider is using."
        },
        {
          "term": "Scale-up domain",
          "definition": "The set of GPUs wired together with high-bandwidth interconnect (e.g. an NVL72 rack of 72 Blackwells) that behave as one large accelerator for the purposes of this analysis."
        }
      ],
