          "plainExplanation": "Both fields analyse their function by asking 'if I nudge the input, what happens to the output', and both fields design around the answer, but they want opposite answers. In cryptography the nudge is a bit-difference: XOR a chosen pattern into the plaintext, encrypt both versions, and XOR the two ciphertexts. There is no calculus here because the arithmetic lives in GF(2) - Reiner's point that 'you have to differentiate with respect to binary numbers'. For an ideal n-bit block cipher every specific output difference should occur with probability about 2^-n, so no input difference tells you anything; a differential attack works precisely when some input-difference-to-output-difference pair happens far more often than 2^-n. Hence Reiner's phrasing that a well-designed cipher makes it 'quite difficult to make the difference of the output be small' and that its optimisation goal is 'complexifying'. A transformer wants the reverse. The residual connection makes the derivative of a block equal to the identity plus the block's own derivative, so by default gradient flows through depth essentially unchanged, and LayerNorm holds the scale of activations and gradients in range. Reiner's 'we do a lot of work to not overcomplicate the derivative' is literally a statement about keeping this Jacobian near the identity. Adversarial examples are what it looks like when a network fails at this and accidentally satisfies the cipher's criterion instead: a tiny input perturbation that flips the output completely.",
