      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "wrongBelief": "Neural nets and ciphers are basically the same kind of object, since both are deep stacks of mixing operations.",
          "correction": "They share the mixing primitive and both are analysed by differentiating the function, but they optimise opposite objectives. A cipher is designed so that a tiny input difference produces a large, unpredictable output difference; a transformer adds residual connections and LayerNorm precisely to keep the input-to-output Jacobian near the identity so the derivative stays tame. They also live in different algebras: GF(2) bits under XOR versus real numbers. Reiner's whole framing is that the resemblance is in the mechanism and the difference is in the sign of the objective."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "An untrained, randomly initialised network is just noise and does nothing interesting.",
          "correction": "Reiner's point runs the other way: random initialisation already jumbles inputs in a complicated way, and such a network 'maybe is a reasonable cipher as well.' What the random network lacks is not scrambling power but a meaningful derivative. Gradient descent, not the mixing architecture, is what converts a scrambler into a learner."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "RevNets eliminate memory usage during training.",
          "correction": "They eliminate the stack of stored intermediate activations that grows linearly with depth, which is often the largest single term, but parameters, gradients, optimizer state and the current layer's working set all remain. In practice normalisation statistics, dropout masks and any genuinely non-reversible ops (embeddings, pooling, the output head) still need saving or deterministic reseeding, the hidden state has to be split into two halves so each f sees half the channels unless you widen the model, and floating-point reversal is not bit-exact, so reconstructed activations drift slightly from the originals."
        },
        {
          "wrongBelief": "RevNets save memory, so they should be the default way to train large models.",
          "correction": "The chapter's closing move is exactly the argument against that. Full rematerialisation costs roughly 33% more FLOPs per step, and Reiner's rule is that on current hardware the profitable direction is the opposite one: spend memory to save compute, as the KV cache does. Cheaper ways to shed activation memory also exist (sharding activations across data-parallel ranks, host offload, selective checkpointing). Elegance is not the deciding factor; the hardware's exchange rate between FLOPs and bytes is."
        }
      ],
      "jargon": [
        {
          "term": "Feistel network (Feistel cipher)",
          "definition": "A construction that turns any function f, even a lossy one you cannot undo, into a perfectly invertible layer. Split the state into halves x and y, output (y + f(x), x), and reverse it by reading x off and subtracting f(x) from the other half. It underpins DES and many other block ciphers, and it is the one idea Reiner credits as crossing cleanly from cryptography into deep learning."
        },
        {
          "term": "Differential cryptanalysis",
          "definition": "The main way of attacking a block cipher by 'differentiating' it: feed in pairs of inputs that differ by a chosen bit pattern and look for output differences that appear more often than chance. A cipher is considered well designed when no such biased input-to-output difference pair can be found."
        },
        {
          "term": "Avalanche property",
          "definition": "The requirement that changing one bit of a cipher's input changes about half the output bits, unpredictably. It is the design goal in cryptography and the exact same behaviour that, in a neural network, shows up as an adversarial example and counts as a bug."
        },
        {
          "term": "GF(2) / the field of two elements",
          "definition": "Arithmetic on single bits where addition is XOR and multiplication is AND. Ciphers operate here, which is why their 'derivative' is a difference of XORs rather than a calculus derivative; neural nets operate on real numbers, where actual differentiation is available."
        },
        {
          "term": "Residual connection",
          "definition": "The skip path that adds a layer's input to its output. Its purpose is arithmetic on the derivative: the Jacobian of the block becomes the identity plus the layer's own Jacobian, so gradients survive a journey through a hundred layers instead of vanishing or exploding."
        },
        {
          "term": "LayerNorm",
          "definition": "A normalisation step that rescales activations to a controlled mean and variance within each token. Like the residual connection, it exists to keep the scale of activations and gradients in a range where gradient descent behaves, not to add expressive power."
        },
        {
          "term": "RevNet (reversible residual network)",
          "definition": "The 2017 paper that applies the Feistel construction to deep networks so the whole forward pass can be run backwards exactly. Its purpose is not accuracy but memory: activations never need to be stored, because they can be reconstructed on the fly."
        },
        {
          "term": "Activations",
          "definition": "The intermediate tensors a network produces at each layer during the forward pass. They must be kept until the backward pass consumes them, which is why their total size scales with depth and dominates training memory."
        },
        {
          "term": "HBM (high-bandwidth memory)",
          "definition": "The fast memory stacked next to an accelerator die, where weights, activations and KV caches live. It is the scarce resource this chapter's whole argument is denominated in: a few tens to a couple of hundred gigabytes per chip."
        },
        {
          "term": "Rematerialisation / gradient checkpointing",
          "definition": "Deliberately discarding activations during the forward pass and recomputing them during the backward pass, buying memory with extra FLOPs. Checkpointing recomputes from stored layer boundaries; RevNets go further and reconstruct by running the network in reverse, needing no stored boundaries at all."
        },
        {
          "term": "Backward pass",
          "definition": "The gradient computation that walks from the loss back to the inputs, costing roughly twice the FLOPs of a forward pass. It is the consumer of stored activations, and therefore the reason activation memory exists at all."
        },
        {
          "term": "Adversarial example",
          "definition": "An input perturbed so slightly that a human sees no change, yet the model's output flips completely. Reiner's point is that this is not an exotic ML pathology but a cipher property showing up where nobody wanted it."
        },
        {
          "term": "Backdoor (in an LLM)",
          "definition": "A hidden behaviour planted during training that fires on a secret trigger. Reiner's framing is that it is not an input to the forward pass but an input to the backward pass, smuggled in through the gradients, which is what makes it hard to spot by inspecting the served model."
        },
        {
          "term": "KV cache",
          "definition": "The stored keys and values for every previous token, kept so that generating each new token requires attending to memory rather than recomputing the entire prefix. It is the archetype of buying compute with memory, and the exact mirror of what RevNets do."
        }
      ],
